  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Several chances for rain this weekend into \nnext week. \n\nSatellite imagery shows a large swath of moisture stretching\nfrom the southern Plains towards the Great Lakes and Mid \nAtlantic regions. Showery activity over the Mid Atlantic was \ncoincident with saturation below 12kft on this morning's KFCX \nsounding. Flow from 925 mb to about 850mb was from the SE, while\nsurface winds were more ENE where high pressure was wedging in.\nThe weak isentropic upglide of higher specific humidity air \nwill continue to support drizzle and showers. Storms are \nextremely unlikely, as this is more of a wintertime stratiform\nset up, so have removed them from the forecast. Moderate rain \nis possible in upslope locations. Overnight, additional short \nwave energy will keep the rain going. On Saturday, another short\nwave pushes through and will bring scattered showers and maybe\nan isolated thunderstorm mainly to points along and east of the\nBlue Ridge. To the west, drier air will be slowly filtering in \nand will limit rainfall. \n\nOn Sunday and Monday, convection looks limited again with drier\nair in place, so most folks should see a break. NW flow aloft \nbrings another round of energy through late Monday night into \nTuesday, with a strong cold front moving through sometime late \nTuesday into early Wednesday. After that, we should get another \nbreak from daily rain chances for at least a couple days. \n\nWe are outlooked for both a 15% chance of severe weather from\nSPC on Day 5 (Tuesday), as well as a Marginal Risk of excessive \nrainfall from WPC with the cold frontal passage. UCAR's medium \nrange convective model is also highlighting Tuesday for a severe\nweather threat, which would consist of damaging winds and heavy\nrain possibly leading to flash flooding.\n\nAfter today, we should see a gradual warm up over the weekend. \nTemperatures reach the low 80s for most mountain locations by \nMonday, and mid to upper 80s for the Piedmont and Roanoke \nregions. Even a few low 90s are possible early next week in \ngusty WSW surface flow for Roanoke and the Piedmont areas.",
